I don't know what the name is for this pattern so I've made one up, but basically the assembly for this function looks awful:
```go
func ConstantTimeCompare(x, y []byte) int {
	if len(x) != len(y) { panic("...") }

	var v byte

	for ; len(x) > 0; x, y = x[1:], y[1:] {
		a := y[0]
		b := x[0]

		v |= a ^ b
	}

	return constanttime.Eq(v, 0)
}
```
- after early loop rotation #77089 we could move `s[1:]` operations into `if len(x) > 0 { /* here */; goto loop }` second loop check, this would allow us to remove the slice mask
- we keep track of the length of both slices, even tho it is identical

### Proposal Details

**Description**:

Since Go 1.25 the compiler has a nice optimization: small slices created via

```go
make([]T, 0, smallSize)
```

often **do not escape to the heap**

This allows very fast zero-allocation cloning in the pattern:

```go
append(make([]byte, 0, len(s)), s...)
```

Unfortunately the current implementation of `slices.Clone` (as of Go 1.26r2 / tip) uses `append([]byte{}, s...)`

https://github.com/golang/go/blob/4606a931d2b099f2c5e5619b797352607ee42225/src/slices/slices.go#L349-L361

**Benchmark comparison** (go version go1.26rc2 linux/amd64, small slice 32 bytes)

```go
type S = []uint8
array := make(S, 32)
```

Results:

```go
Benchmark/slices.Clone          10000         39.13 ns/op       32 B/op        1 allocs/op
Benchmark/MyClone               10000         3.922 ns/op        0 B/op        0 allocs/op
```

Full benchmark code:

```go
package main

import (
	"slices"
	"testing"
)

func MyClone[S ~[]E, E any](s S) S {
	if s == nil {
		return nil
	}
	return append(make(S, 0, len(s)), s...)
}

func Benchmark(b *testing.B) {
	type S = []uint8
	array := make(S, 32)

	b.Run("slices.Clone", func(b *testing.B) {
		for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
			_ = slices.Clone(array)
		}
	})

	b.Run("MyClone", func(b *testing.B) {
		for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
			_ = MyClone(array)
		}
	})
}
```

**Proposal**

Change the implementation of `slices.Clone` to use the pre-allocating pattern so it can benefit from the same escape-analysis + stack allocation optimization that has been available since 1.25 for small slices:

```go
func Clone[S ~[]E, E any](s S) S {
	if s == nil {
		return nil
	}
	// len(s) is small → very often stack allocated
	return append(make(S, 0, len(s)), s...)
}
```

Or, more generally — make `append` itself work without memory allocation (in this pattern).

